# The Return of Zero — Revised Production and Publication Protocol

**Status:** Second draft for Frank’s review  
**Authority:** Non-governing proposal  
**Scope:** Replacement sections governing phase separation, multi-model execution, publication ontology, deep records, reciprocal linking, and running order.

## 1. Two distinct programmes

The work is divided into two programmes with different purposes, branches, models, and completion criteria.

### Programme A — Preliminary construction

Preliminary work prepares a stable writing environment. It does not draft the essay and does not perform quilt harmonisation.

Its objects are:

- repository and branch safety;
- test and graph integrity;
- stable identifiers and manuscript anchors;
- the multi-model execution contract;
- writing-packet generation;
- the public-vault ontology;
- record schemas and relation types;
- publication filtering;
- diagram and asset conventions;
- navigation and point-back machinery.

Preliminary prose is operational specification only. It is not candidate manuscript prose and should not enter the writing models’ calibration field.

### Programme B — Actual writing

Writing begins only from an accepted canonical baseline received from the separate quilting and propagation session.

Its objects are:

- the continuous manuscript;
- station and movement prose;
- reader-facing Symbolon, Matheme, Mytheme, and Episteme records;
- source and history treatments;
- argument and essay maps;
- figures, plates, captions, and media relations;
- section-to-section flow;
- whole-manuscript recursive rereading;
- public-vault composition and release.

The transition between the programmes is marked by an immutable writing-baseline commit and tag. Nothing produced during preliminary work becomes manuscript prose merely because it is already available.

## 2. Quilt boundary

Quilt work is removed from this protocol’s preliminary running order.

Another session owns:

- harmonising the quilt;
- Frank’s acceptance docket;
- propagation into the central plan;
- propagation into arguments, concepts, paths, and section nodes;
- regeneration of affected canonical projections.

This protocol receives that work only as an incoming canonical baseline.

Writing is blocked until the incoming baseline contains:

- the accepted governing plan;
- updated canonical argument and concept nodes;
- updated transverse paths;
- all 48 movement nodes in their accepted state;
- fresh generated rooms and source projections;
- a recorded list of decisions still deliberately open.

The writing branches do not reopen quilt adjudication. If drafting exposes a genuine canonical problem, it is returned to the canonical integration branch as a named proposal. Every model branch then receives the same corrected baseline before comparative drafting resumes.

## 3. Branch and worktree architecture

The branch structure makes preliminary activity, model experiments, review, and selected manuscript prose visibly distinct.

```text
main
  └── codex/preliminary-foundation
          │
          └── accepted preliminary merge
                  │
                  └── writing-baseline-v1
                         ├── codex/draft-<model-a>
                         ├── codex/draft-<model-b>
                         ├── codex/draft-<model-c>
                         └── codex/written-edition
```

### 3.1 Preliminary branch

`codex/preliminary-foundation` contains only infrastructure required before writing:

- test and path repairs;
- execution manifests;
- context-packet tooling;
- publication ontology;
- public-vault builders and filters;
- stable-ID and point-back contracts;
- record validation;
- diagram and navigation specifications.

It does not contain speculative manuscript prose.

After review, it merges into the accepted development baseline. That baseline is tagged—for example:

`writing-baseline-v1`

### 3.2 Model writing branches

Every writing model receives its own branch and isolated worktree. Parallel models never share an active working directory or switch the branch beneath another run.

Each branch:

- begins at the identical writing-baseline commit;
- is assigned one declared model;
- receives the same station packet;
- receives the same writing prompt and stopping condition;
- has the same write permissions and file scope;
- runs the same review and verification commands;
- remains isolated from the other models’ prose until its independent draft is complete.

Model selection is performed explicitly when the run is launched. The repository does not pretend to select or enforce a model invisibly.

### 3.3 Integration branch

`codex/written-edition` is the only branch from which manuscript prose is accepted for the finished edition.

Whole model branches are not merged automatically. For each station:

1. independent drafts complete;
2. drafts are compared under the same rubric;
3. findings are recorded without model prestige deciding the result;
4. Frank selects a draft, selects operations from several drafts, or rejects them all;
5. the selected movement is revised as one continuous piece;
6. only the accepted station lands on `codex/written-edition`.

The integration branch must never become a stitched anthology of model voices. Selection happens at the level of argument, movement, image, transition, and sentence—not by mechanically combining every apparently strong paragraph.

## 4. Equalised multi-model execution

A multi-model comparison is meaningful only when model identity is the intended variable.

Every writing run receives the same execution manifest.

### 4.1 Fixed execution fields

The following remain identical across comparative branches:

- base commit and tag;
- task and station identifier;
- prompt version;
- project instructions and local skills;
- complete context-packet paths and hashes;
- manuscript frontier;
- source and passage set;
- accepted authorial materials;
- visual brief;
- allowed write surfaces;
- tool permissions;
- internet and source-retrieval policy;
- reasoning-effort level where the runtime supports it;
- time or turn budget;
- review stages;
- rubric version;
- test commands;
- completion and stop conditions;
- required handoff format.

If two runtimes expose different parameter names, the protocol uses the closest common semantic level and records the actual values. A hidden parameter difference must not be mistaken for a model-quality difference.

### 4.2 Variable execution field

For a controlled writing comparison, the intended variable is:

- model identity.

A later experiment may deliberately vary context, prompt, or reviewer, but that becomes a differently named experiment and does not enter the same comparison table.

### 4.3 Context equality

