- displays a figure, topology, map, or plate;
- qualifies or tests a manuscript claim;
- enables a reader to reconstruct the whole from a local aperture.

A page is not admitted because related material happens to exist. Every record must change what the reader can understand and point back to the exact passage whose depth it opens.

## 7. Submission ontology: Symbolon containing Matheme, Mytheme, and Episteme

The public-vault ontology is now explicit rather than merely role-based.

The published vault presents:

```text
START-HERE.md
THE-RETURN-OF-ZERO.md

symbolon/
  SYMBOLON.md

  sections/
  relations/
  paths/
  plates/

  matheme/
    MATHEME.md
    ql/
    spanda/
    number/
    logic/
    geometry/
    topology/
    harmonics/
    computation/
    figures/

  mytheme/
    MYTHEME.md
    myths/
    narratives/
    poetry/
    media/
    artworks/
    encounters/
    figures/

  episteme/
    EPISTEME.md
    sources/
    histories/
    lenses/
    methods/
    essay-maps/
    argument-maps/
    source-maps/
    figures/
```

The exact lower-level folders may grow from the real material. The four primary relations do not change:

- `symbolon` contains the field;
- `matheme` carries exact transmissible form;
- `mytheme` carries lived, narrative, imaginal, poetic, and artistic form;
- `episteme` carries established knowledge, sources, histories, lenses, methods, and maps.

This explicitly revises the present submission specification’s “roles, not mandatory folders” position. That older position remains useful for discovery inside the development corpus, but the public reader surface now uses this ontology because the ontology itself carries the philosophical relation.

## 8. The office of Symbolon

`symbolon/` is not a fourth content bucket placed beside the others. It is their containing and relating field.

`SYMBOLON.md` is the reader’s meta-entry into:

- how a broken or differentiated part retains its absent counterpart;
- how the three registers belong together without becoming identical;
- how local records point outwards and return;
- how linear reading opens into holographic reading;
- how the essay’s parts reconstruct their whole.

### 8.1 Symbolon’s own core records

The `relations/` layer carries the concepts and dynamics that perform the gathering itself:

- `0/1`;
- `1/0`;
- the slash as differentiating and relating activity;
- self-identity and self-difference;
- Mono and Poly;
- the two ones;
- dia-ballein and sym-ballein;
- complexio oppositorum;
- opposition, conjunction, inversion, superposition, and return;
- the immutable gap;
- `5→0`;
- the eight determinations;
- the distinction between parent relation, sixfold body, and return-switch;
- the symbolon as fracture, authentication, and recognition;
- local whole, global whole, and holographic reconstruction.

These records may contain formal expressions, myths, and sources, but their primary office is cross-register gathering. Their mathematical derivations point into Matheme; their narrative and artistic disclosures point into Mytheme; their histories and warrants point into Episteme.

### 8.2 Sections as local symbola

The modular station and movement pages live under `symbolon/sections/` because every section gathers the three registers around one local proposition.

A section record contains:

- threshold and inheritance;
- governing proposition;
- sixfold movement;
- principal matheme;
- principal mytheme;
- principal episteme;
- achieved symbolon;
- outgoing movement;
- exact links back to the sovereign essay;
- exact links into its deeper records.

A section page does not duplicate the manuscript. It makes the local generative order traversable.

### 8.3 Paths and plates

`symbolon/paths/` carries reader paths that cross registers and return.

`symbolon/plates/` carries plates whose argument depends on the coordinated presence of Matheme, Mytheme, and Episteme. A purely formal diagram lives primarily in Matheme; a mythic or artistic figure lives primarily in Mytheme; a source or historical map lives primarily in Episteme.

## 9. Matheme records

Matheme carries the exact formal and transmissible operations of the work.

Its primary materials include:

- the Spanda equations;
- the `4+2` and `3:3` accountings;
- QL theorem units;
- the two logics in their formal expressions;
- signed and slashed operations;
- zero, empty set, and ordinal constructions;
- complex orientation and rotation;
- projective completion;
- quotient and covering topology;
- torus and winding;
- harmonic ratios and musical remainder;
- formal incompleteness under its exact scope;
- computational process operations;
- technical diagrams whose formal transformations can be stated and checked.

The eight determinations have their primary gathering record in Symbolon. Matheme carries their exact subsidiary derivations without creating a second competing “eight determinations” page.

Every Matheme record distinguishes:

- what is mathematically Derived;
- what is Derived only within declared QL operations;
- what cross-register use is Argued;
- what remains Offered.

A formula never acquires a historical, psychological, theological, or political meaning merely through resemblance.

## 10. Mytheme records

Mytheme carries the forms in which a relation is lived, remembered, suffered, narrated, enacted, or made perceptible.

Its primary materials include:

- Ares, Aphrodite, Harmonia, and Hephaestus;
- Apollo, Daphne, Dionysus, Eros, and Psyche;
- Indra’s net and other admitted cross-cultural images;
- poems and short authorial forms;
- narrative scenes;
- first-person encounters;
- film, television, music, digital media, and cultural references;
- artworks and artistic methods;
- travelling images such as the jigsaw, wheel and thread, stained glass, mirror, wound, hole, net, mechanism, and atlas;
- media objects that reveal how an argument is staged socially or technologically.

Each Mytheme record states:

- what operation the image performs;
- where the essay earns it;
- what changes when the reader encounters it;
- its source and provenance;
- what it does not prove;
- whether it is Extracted, Paraphrased, Argued from, or Resonant with;
- where the reader returns to the essay.

Frank’s poems remain verbatim authorial works. They can operate as records, insets, hinges, audible anchors, or plate language, but never as disposable illustrations of prose written elsewhere.

## 11. Episteme records

Episteme carries the instituted, historical, scholarly, methodological, and navigational knowledge-field.

### 11.1 Sources

Every public source record derives from one canonical `SOURCE.md`.

A public source record carries only publication-safe material:

- bibliographic identity;
- edition;
- a concise account of the work;
- verified passages where lawful;
- citation and quotation readiness;
- the work’s actual contribution;
- limiting or qualifying material;
- exact essay and argument consumers;
- links back to the passages it bears.

Raw private notes and rights-uncleared source material do not ship.

### 11.2 Histories

History records are developed argumentative routes, not chronological dumps.

They may include:

- histories of zero and number;
- histories of logic and formal limit;
- Indian philosophical lineages;
- Buddhist and Śaiva differentiations;
- psychology and psychoanalysis;
- process and systems thought;
- mythic transmission;
- language, symbol, and dialogue;
- technology, computation, governance, and political economy;
- the history of the subject’s changing place in knowledge.

Each history record distinguishes:

- primary material;
- later interpretation;
- Frank’s argument;
- unresolved provenance or acquisition debt;
- the exact sections whose movement it historicises.

### 11.3 MEF lenses and methods

MEF lenses live under Episteme because they are disciplined ways of knowing and refracting a field.

Each lens record states:

- its question;
- its operation;
- its appropriate evidence;
- what it makes legible;
- what it cannot establish;
- where it is active in the essay;
- which other lenses supply the necessary counter-reading or return.

The lenses do not become a separate public theory competing with QL. They are the epistemic articulation of how the Symbolon is read through differentiated conditions.

### 11.4 Essay and argument maps

Essay maps, argument maps, source maps, and research maps live under Episteme.

A map is a knowledge instrument. It shows declared relations and never manufactures relations from common tags or vocabulary.

Maps may display:

- station sequence;
- argument dependencies;
- source-to-claim relations;
- transverse paths;
- register crossings;
- evidence and qualification boundaries;
- figure placement;
- unresolved questions.

The map points into the work. It does not replace the prose or become a second canon.

## 12. Incorporating the canonical node types

The development corpus retains its existing authoritative node types. Publication maps them into the Symbolon ontology without erasing their roles.

| Canonical type | Primary public home |
|---|---|
| Master manuscript | Vault root |
| Section or movement | `symbolon/sections/` |
| Cross-register core concept | `symbolon/relations/` |
| Transverse reader path | `symbolon/paths/` |
| Cross-register plate | `symbolon/plates/` |
| Formal theorem or derivation | `symbolon/matheme/` |
| Myth, poem, narrative, media, artwork, encounter | `symbolon/mytheme/` |
| Source, history, MEF lens, method, essay map, argument map | `symbolon/episteme/` |
| Argument node | The register in which its primary operation occurs |
| Concept node | The register in which its primary operation occurs |
| Figure | The register of its argumentative job |

Every record has one primary home. Cross-register relevance is carried through typed links, not duplicated bodies.

The publication hierarchy does not change canonical authority:

- plan remains governing;
- nodes retain granular argument authority;
- `SOURCE.md` remains evidence authority;
- the manuscript remains sovereign;
- public records remain submission refractions.

## 13. Record contract

Every public record contains enough prose to be read, not merely indexed.

Required fields include:

```yaml
id:
title:
record_type:
register:
authority: submission-refraction
claim_status:
canonical_source:
points_back_to:
bears_on:
relations:
sources:
```

Fields appear only where meaningful. A poem does not need to masquerade as an argument node; a source does not need a QL coordinate merely to satisfy a template.

### 13.1 Typed relations

Relations use a small, explicit vocabulary:

- `derives`;
- `defines`;
- `evidences`;
- `historicises`;
- `figures`;
- `refracts`;
- `qualifies`;
- `applies`;
- `maps`;
- `opens`;
- `returns_to`.

The relation is declared at the point where its meaning can be stated. Tags and folder adjacency never create an edge.

### 13.2 Record body

A developed deep record normally contains:

1. the proposition, work, image, or source encountered;
2. the operation it performs;
3. its full local development;
4. its relation to neighbouring records;
5. its exact relation to the manuscript and argument;
6. its source and status boundary;
7. a return route.

No reader-facing page ships as a title, metadata card, or fluent but non-functional summary.

## 14. Granular pointing-out and pointing-back

The vault’s central navigational law is reciprocity.

When the essay points outward, the deeper record points back to the exact place from which the reader came.

### 14.1 Essay-to-record relation

Every outward link is framed in prose:

- what lies behind the link;
- why it matters here;
- whether it deepens, derives, historicises, figures, qualifies, or applies the claim.

The essay never says only “see also.”

### 14.2 Record-to-essay relation

Every deep record contains a visible “Return to the essay” movement linking to:

- the exact station;
- the exact movement;
- the exact heading or block;
- the argument node where relevant.

A source record points back to every public claim it actually bears. A history record points back to the movements it historicises. A poem or myth points back to the passage whose operation it makes live. A formal derivation points back to the sentence whose compression it unfolds.

### 14.3 Stable anchors

Stable block or heading IDs are assigned before the deep records are finalised.

A point-back target must survive:

- heading edits;
- filename changes;
- title revisions;
- public-vault regeneration.

Broken point-back relations block publication.

### 14.4 Tangential depth

Tangential pieces are not loose digressions. They are lateral apertures with disciplined return.

Every tangential record answers:

- What becomes visible here that the main line cannot carry in full?
- Which exact manuscript operation opened this route?
- Which canonical argument governs it?
- Which source or authorial material bears it?
- What changes when the reader returns?

This creates depth without allowing the essay to dissolve into an archive.

## 15. Page-level minigraphs

Every public record receives a compact minigraph generated from its explicit typed relations.

The current page sits at the centre. The graph may show:

- the manuscript passage that opened it;
- its containing register;
- its governing Symbolon record;
- one or two primary dependencies;
- one or two downstream consequences;
- its return target.

A section record may additionally show previous and next movement.

The visible graph remains small. A readable link list carries the same relations for accessibility and Obsidian compatibility.

Minigraphs do not infer semantic relations. They render the relations the records already declare.

## 16. Revised running order

### Preliminary programme

1. Preserve and review the current repository state.
2. Establish the development remote and accepted checkpoint.
3. Create `codex/preliminary-foundation`.
4. Repair the test, path, graph, adapter, and source-anchor baseline.
5. Define stable asset and manuscript-anchor IDs.
6. Define the multi-model execution manifest.
7. Build and test identical context-packet generation.
8. Establish isolated branch/worktree creation.
9. Amend the publication specification to make `symbolon/` the containing public ontology.
10. Define the Matheme, Mytheme, and Episteme record contracts.
11. Define canonical-node-to-public-record routing.
12. Define typed relations and granular point-back requirements.
13. Define minigraph generation from declared relations.
14. Define publication filters, source-rights rules, and clean-repository tests.
15. Receive the accepted canonical baseline from the separate quilting session.
16. Verify it without reopening quilt work.
17. Merge accepted preliminary infrastructure.
18. Create and tag `writing-baseline-v1`.
19. Create identical model writing branches and isolated worktrees.

### Actual writing programme

1. Generate the §0/1 writing packet.
2. Run equalised independent model drafts.
3. Review, compare, select, revise, and integrate §0/1.
4. Author its accepted Symbolon, Matheme, Mytheme, and Episteme records.
5. Add its exact outward and return links.
6. Build its admitted plates and figures.
7. Repeat the complete cycle for §0.
8. Perform the first whole-manuscript recursive return.
9. Repeat for §1 and §2.
10. Perform the second recursive return.
11. Repeat for §3 and §4.
12. Perform the third recursive return.
13. Repeat for §5 and §5→0.
14. Perform the full linear reread.
15. Perform the radial, transverse, toroidal, and holographic rereads.
16. Complete all histories, source records, lenses, maps, tangential pieces, plates, and point-back relations.
17. Generate the public vault through the Symbolon ontology.
18. Verify every record, link, return, minigraph, figure, source boundary, and asset.
19. Test the vault from a clean clone.
20. Publish the public repository and Obsidian surface.
21. Prepare the submission description, abstract, and supporting artifacts from the finished work.
:::

