# The Return of Zero — Multi-Model Writing and Symbolon Publication Protocol

**Status:** Revised first draft for Frank’s review  
**Authority:** Non-governing proposal  
**Scope:** Preliminary preparation, controlled model-branch writing, recursive review, selection, and public Obsidian publication  
**Excluded workflow:** Quilt harmonisation and propagation, which is being completed in another session

## 0. Governing outcome

The work will produce:

1. one continuous, sovereign essay;
2. independently written model versions made from the same canonical base and under the same execution contract;
3. a modular depth-field organised by the ontology of the work itself;
4. a clean public Obsidian repository whose principal architecture is:

```text
THE-RETURN-OF-ZERO.md
symbolon/
  INDEX.md
  relations/
  matheme/
  mytheme/
  episteme/
```

The main essay performs the argument linearly. The `symbolon/` body opens that argument holographically.

The three registers are not decorative categories:

- `matheme/` carries exact operations, derivations, formal constructions, topology, mathematical music, and QL foundations;
- `mytheme/` carries myth, narrative, poetry, media, images, artworks, scenes, and lived or cultural enactments;
- `episteme/` carries sources, histories, scholarly records, MEF lenses, argument maps, essay maps, technical dossiers, and public warrants;
- `symbolon/relations/` gathers the cross-register relations through which these records belong to one work.

The ontology therefore carries the content. Folder placement states what kind of work a record performs, while metadata preserves its more granular node type, status, sources, targets, and relations.

## 1. Three distinct phases

The protocol separates three kinds of activity.

### Phase P — Preliminary preparation on `main`

This phase prepares canon, repository health, publication schema, model execution contracts, writing packets, and branches.

It produces no manuscript prose, no model-version prose, and no finished public depth records.

### Phase W — Writing on model-specific branches

Each designated model writes a complete version on its own branch from the same base commit and under the same execution parameters.

No worktrees are used. Model branches are checked out and run sequentially.

### Phase S — Selection, integration, and publication

The completed model versions are compared under the same rubric. Frank selects the primary version and any limited imports from other branches. The selected work is integrated, re-audited, merged, and projected into the public `symbolon/` vault.

The three phases must not bleed into one another. Preliminary work does not quietly draft the essay; writing branches do not redesign canon or the publication schema; publication does not become another opportunity to rewrite the argument without returning through the writing gates.

---

# PART I — PRELIMINARY PREPARATION ON `main`

## P0. Canonical handoff boundary

The current `main` is receiving substantial work from the separate quilt session, including changes to the plan, theorem spine, histories, arguments, concepts, paths, sources, and generated rooms.

Preliminary preparation begins only when that session hands over:

- a named canonical completion commit;
- a clean working tree;
- current generated rooms and source projections;
- a passing or explicitly debt-accounted graph state;
- a clear list of unresolved authorial decisions;
- the revised central plan and granular nodes that the writing branches are to inherit.

This protocol does not inspect, adjudicate, schedule, or repeat the quilt process. Its only concern is the quality of the canonical handoff.

The writing branches never consume the quilt itself. They consume:

- the central plan;
- orienting principles;
- theorem spine;
- accepted section, argument, concept, history, and path nodes;
- canonical sources;
- generated writing contexts;
- the sovereign manuscript skeleton;
- approved authorial writings and visual briefs.

## P1. Protect the canonical baseline

Once the other session finishes:

1. Confirm that the working tree is clean.
2. Confirm that no protected `NOTES.md` has been staged or altered by an agent.
3. Confirm that generated files correspond to their canonical inputs.
4. Record the exact handoff commit.
5. Tag or otherwise name this commit as the common writing baseline.
6. Do not amend or rewrite this baseline after model branches have begun.

Every model branch starts from this exact commit. If canon changes materially after branching, either:

- all model runs restart from the new common commit; or
- the changed run is declared a different experiment and is not compared as though its inputs were identical.

No model branch rebases or merges from a changing `main` midway through its writing run.

## P2. Repository health gate

The real workspace must be checked after the quilt-session handoff:

```text
source projections current
section rooms current
OKF graph checked
all source links resolved or explicitly debt-accounted
BKMR adapters current where used
full test suite run
submission-package tests run
clean-clone checks prepared
```

Tests protect real functionality. A failing test is investigated individually.

The repair rules are:

- update stale path expectations to the actual domain/author/source layout;
- preserve the behaviour the test was meant to protect;
- never delete, skip, mock, or weaken a test merely to produce green output;
- distinguish obsolete assumptions from genuine graph, source, freshness, or publication failures;
- keep canonical Markdown authoritative and generated indexes disposable.

The preliminary gate closes only when:

- the real writing and source commands work;
- source and room projections are current;
- the graph can recover bounded writing contexts;
- every declared source and path relation needed by drafting resolves;
- the suite is green, or Frank has accepted a precisely bounded non-writing debt that cannot affect the model comparison.

## P3. Git topology and branch creation

### Development repository

The current repository remains the canonical development workspace. It contains the full argument system, source bank, histories, tools, tests, rooms, manuscript, and publication builder.

A remote is configured only after checking:

- repository visibility;
- secrets and personal data;
- authorial notes;
- copyrighted source material;
- licence implications;
- unwanted files in prior Git history.

The public reader repository is preferably a separate deterministic projection. Making the full development repository public would expose all branches and history, not merely the final vault.

### Writing branches

After the common baseline is fixed, create one branch for each writing model:

```text
codex/write-<model-slug>
```

For example:

```text
codex/write-model-a
codex/write-model-b
codex/write-model-c
```

The actual branch names should record stable model identifiers rather than marketing nicknames where possible.

Branch creation occurs from `main`, but manuscript writing does not occur on `main`.

No worktrees are used.

The branches are run sequentially:

1. check out the model branch;
2. verify the branch and base commit;
3. run the complete writing protocol;
4. commit and close that version;
5. return to the common baseline;
6. check out the next model branch.

No concurrent subagent may write while another branch is checked out. Review subagents within one branch are either read-only or are used sequentially under one clearly assigned write owner.

## P4. Common execution contract

Every model receives the same execution contract. The model identifier is the intended variable.

The contract records:

- protocol version;
- common base commit;
- designated model ID;
- branch name;
- reasoning-effort setting where the runtime exposes one;
- service tier where configurable;
- tool and network permissions;
- context-compaction rules;
- section order;
- canonical packet builder and version;
- packet hashes or source commit;
- fresh-review roles;
- allowed and prohibited files;
- stopping conditions;
- test commands;
- writing-guidance version;
- publication-schema version;
- final output expectations.

The following remain identical across branches:

- user brief;
- project instructions;
- writing laws;
- rubric;
- source and authority rules;
- station order;
- recursive-reading procedure;
- fresh-review procedure;
- source packet;
- accepted authorial material;
- visual brief;
- word-count target, if Frank sets one;
- section completion criteria;
- whole-manuscript acceptance criteria.

Parameters that the runtime does not actually expose—such as a hidden temperature control—are not invented or claimed.

Project files do not claim dependable per-subagent model routing. The designated model is selected by the external session or runtime configuration. Where subagent model selection is controllable, branch-internal reviewers use the same designated model for a clean within-model run. Where it is not controllable, the actual reviewer model is recorded in the run manifest.

Freshness always means a new context. It never means pretending that an old context has forgotten its prior defence of the prose.

## P5. Preliminary writing infrastructure

Preliminary work prepares the instruments of writing without writing the manuscript.

It establishes:

- the station and movement order;
- the exact context-packet procedure;
- stable manuscript anchors;
- public record IDs;
- source and passage retrieval;
- review report format;
- figure-brief format;
- public ontology schema;
- link and minigraph generation;
- branch run manifests;
- test fixtures using real project material.

Optional `SCRATCH.md` or `VISUALS.md` surfaces are created only if Frank admits them. They are never required merely because a workflow expects files.

Generated `ROOM.md` files remain waypoints. They are not drafting authorities and are never edited directly.

